; A .rules file that loads the standard library and still means what it says.
;
; The whole point is the word `relation`. Forms expand in every file type, so a
; stdlib that declared `(form (relation R A B) …)` would rewrite the two lines
; below into type declarations and the rules vocabulary would quietly stop
; existing the moment an author loaded the library. The span construction is
; named `span` for exactly that reason (extension §10), and this file is what
; notices if it is ever renamed back.
;
; The declarations that stdlib.writ brings in — the `quiver` and `olog` schemas
; — are skipped here rather than rejected: a loaded library carries
; declarations, and a .rules file asks questions.

(load "stdlib.writ")

(relation subordinate (person person))

(rule (subordinate X Y)
  (is X.reports-to Y))

(rule (subordinate X Y)
  (is X.reports-to Z)
  (subordinate Z Y))
